            {
              "$type": "Models.Core.Folder, Models",
              "ShowInDocs": true,
              "GraphsPerPage": 6,
              "Name": "Iversen",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [
                {
                  "$type": "Models.Factorial.Experiment, Models",
                  "DisabledSimNames": null,
                  "Name": "Iversen",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                      "Text": "This simulation setup was based on a dataset supplied by Hamish Brown, which was collected for his PhD thesis.  The trial was conducted at the Iversen field of Lincoln University, in Lincoln, New Zealand between 1996 and 2002.  The work was focused on understanding water use and its interaction with yield of dryland and irrigated forage crops (lucerne, chicory and red clover were used).  \r\nThe trial was implemented in spring of 1996, after conventional cultivation, including subsoiling and liming.  Chicory (cultivar '*Puna*') was sown at a rate of 3.5 kg/ha and was grazed regularly by sheep.  There were annual applications of superphosphate but no N fertilisation (except excreta from grazing sheep).  The treatments consisted of  irrigation versus dryland conditions.  The major parameters analysed were herbage growth and yield, population, nutritive characteristics, and water utilisation.  \r\nSoil data was obtained from measurements presented by Brown (2004) and complemented by S-MAP database from Landcare Research Limited. Weather data was obtained from the nearby Broadfields met station in Lincoln. \r\n\r\nReferences:  \r\nBrown, H.E. 2004. Understanding yield and water use of dryland forage crops in New Zealand. PhD thesis, Lincoln University, Lincoln, NZ. 288 p.  \r\nBrown, H.E.; Moot, D.J.; & Pollock, K.M. 2003. Long term growth rates and water extraction patterns of dryland chicory, lucerne and red clover. Legumes for dryland pastures. New Zealand Grassland Association, Research Practice Series, 11:91-99.  \r\nBrown, H.E.; Moot, D.J.; & Pollock, K.M. 2005. Herbage production, persistence, nutritive characteristics and water use of perennial forages grown over 6 years on a Wakanui silt loam. New Zealand Journal of Agricultural Research, 48(4):423-439.\r\n",
                      "Name": "Memo",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
